ke the money they say  it's fun they like the social aspect but  it is the flexibility that's key  flexibility is great but you do need  some of those other things that you  mentioned so who are the people that are  most attracted to these jobs well the  millennials primarily i mean they're the  ones who are big in this game but we are  also seeing a growing percentage of the  pre-retirees and the retirees get in too  so about 10 percent of pre-retire  pre-retiree households and 11 percent of  retiree households are actually in the  game too and they like it for those same  reasons but again it is the social  aspect primarily for them they like  making a little extra money and they  don't have the financial independent  kids at home yeah but some people say  this this new economy is kind of like  the wild west that there's no real  regulation here i mean  that's the problem so are we going to  get there do you think some some are  calling for oversight complete  regulation that you see in the  traditional labor market there's talk  about portable benefits for example lots  of different balls in the air i don't  know we'll actually  get there right away i think the  regulatory changes that are being talked  about sort of a long way to go we're  sort of in unchartered territory here  but i think at some point  the employees are going to have to  change their staffing strategies because  they've got to stay competitive so  i don't know what'll happen do you think  these types of jobs will eventually just  take over and replace the traditional  job well if you look at the gig economy  as a whole it is growing faster than the  traditional payroll employment and in  some sectors and in some areas it is  superseding it is the primary mode of  employment so it'd be interesting to see  how this actually shakes out  particularly when you have these new  technologies coming on board you've got  this the the self-driving cars you've  got all these different technologies um  drones  artificial intelligence so what will the  gig economy look like down the line  nobody knows 10 years from now it's  going to be very different  it's just not going away though i think  it's just going to explode yeah 10 years  ago we didn't have uber exactly all  right eric gibbons thanks so much for  being with us this morning

